<b><span fgcolor="#444" size="200%">Auto-heading links​<span font="@dest=Lu0dest=Tl1dest=Hu1" fgcolor="#FFF">​</span>​<span font="@dest=Lu3dest=Tl1dest=Hu1" fgcolor="#FFF">​</span>​<span font="@dest=Lu5dest=Tl1dest=Hu1" fgcolor="#FFF">​</span></span></b>
The auto-heading link feature automatically constructs a CommonMark link reference definition for the display text of each heading in the document. Then authors can add a <i>regular link</i> to jump to the heading. The link destination must start with <tt><span fgcolor="#B56">#</span></tt> (same as the in-page links of HTML documents). If the title includes spaces, wrap the destination with matching pointed brackets.
Example of a regular link:
<tt><span bgcolor="#FFF" fgcolor="#B56"># This heading title
                           ┌── destination ──┐
[Jump to &quot;Heading Title&quot;](&lt;#This heading title&gt;)</span></tt>
In addition to jumping to the heading with a regular link, one can jump with a <i>reference link</i> as the <span font="@dest=Lu9dest=Tl13" fgcolor="#048" underline="single">specification</span> explains.
Examples of reference links:
<tt><span bgcolor="#FFF" fgcolor="#B56">[This heading title]
[Jump to title][This heading title]</span></tt>
The reference link destination must exactly match the heading text, omitting any leading <tt><span fgcolor="#B56">#</span></tt>.
<b>Important:</b> Heading text collisions are not managed. If two headings share the same display text, all reference links to that text will direct to the first matching heading in the document.
⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯⎯
